  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nPer latest GOES East mid-level water vapor imagery, shortwave energy \nis currently advecting through the Ohio Valley into the northern \nTennessee valley, with scattered precipitation locally. This is \nlargely light rain for most locations, however, a few surface \nobservations across higher elevations of southwest Virginia and the \nEast Tennessee mountains remain at or just below freezing, promoting \nsnow showers. Latest snow depth sensor readings from Newfound Gap place\namounts near 2.3\" as of 2 AM. Scattered precipitation will \ntaper off through the mid to late morning as saturation gradually\nshallows out among continued northwest flow.\n\nOverall, the current SPS/Advisory products look to be in good shape, \nand no changes are being made. Bulk of the snowfall accumulations \nare expected to occur at elevations 3000ft or greater, and generally \nexpected to be 4 inches or less. However, localized higher totals \ncannot be ruled out in the highest peaks of the smokies such as Mt. \nLeConte or Newfound Gap. The advisory currently extends until 1PM \nEST. If the latest trends in CAMs hold true, it is possible the \nadvisory may be able to be expired a little earlier, but will hold \noff on any adjustments to end time at this moment. \n\nWhile precip comes to an end today, temperatures will remain around \n10-15 degrees below normal though Sunday. The upper level trough \nwill gradually eject northeastward Monday and into next week, \nleading way to an expected warming trend. In fact, temperatures will \nrebound to well above normal by the second half of the week as \nguidance agrees on an anomalous ridge building across the southeast. \nAn extended period of dry conditions is also expected for the new \nwork week.",
